  "correct": "C",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Work in one quadrant first",
      "body": "<p>In the first quadrant both coordinates are nonnegative. Remove the absolute-value signs there and find where that part of the graph meets the axes.</p>",
      "recap": "The algebraic form of an absolute value depends on the sign of its input.",
      "pitfall": "Draw only the segment allowed by the quadrant, not the entire supporting line.",
      "pause": "Find the two intercepts of the first-quadrant part and sketch the segment between them.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-function-mapping",
          "reminder": "The algebraic form of an absolute value depends on the sign of its input."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use the symmetry to enclose the region",
      "body": "<p>Changing either coordinate to its negative leaves the original equation unchanged. Reflect your segment across the axes, then divide the enclosed region into triangles.</p>",
      "recap": "Axis symmetry can turn one correctly drawn piece into a complete modulus graph.",
      "pitfall": "The question asks for enclosed area, not the total length of the boundary.",
      "pause": "Calculate one triangle area from your intercepts and assemble the total enclosed area.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l12",
          "reminder": "Axis symmetry can turn one correctly drawn piece into a complete modulus graph."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1. Remove the modulus signs by region.</strong> In the first quadrant, the equation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Its relevant segment joins <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2. Reflect the segment.</strong> The equation is unchanged by either <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The full boundary therefore has vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and encloses a square.</p><p><strong>3. Calculate the area.</strong> The axes split this square into four right triangles. Each has area <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></math>, so the total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>Answer C.</strong> Equivalently, the square has diagonals of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, giving area <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
